{
  "header": [
    "SENTENCE REQUESTS — ENGLISH AND NORWEGIAN ONLY. No isiXhosa is written in this file.",
    "",
    "Each request is a sentence a beginner needs, drafted by a model (Claude) in English",
    "and Norwegian around the words its skill already teaches. `words` names those words",
    "by their lexicon lemma, verbatim; `molo content sentence-requests` resolves each one",
    "against the lexicon and reports any it cannot find, rather than guessing. Write",
    "`lemma:pos` when the lexicon has one lemma under two parts of speech (hayi, uxolo, nye).",
    "",
    "The isiXhosa is typed by the tutor in the dashboard (/edit/write) and by nobody",
    "else. Her answer becomes a `draft` sentence with provenance 'tutor, <name>, <date>',",
    "and runs the ordinary sentence gate: tokens checked against xh-morph or marked",
    "irregular with a note, native audio, and a second editor to publish.",
    "",
    "THE ENGLISH IS A PROMPT, NOT A TRANSLATION TO MATCH. If the natural isiXhosa says",
    "something slightly different, the tutor edits the English to say what her sentence",
    "says. If a request does not work in isiXhosa at all, she sets it aside with a reason.",
    "`note` tells her who says it to whom; it is model-written too and may be wrong.",
    "",
    "NORWEGIAN IS UNREVIEWED. Every `nb` string here was drafted by Claude and needs a",
    "Codex review pass (the project rules, 'Norwegian goes to Codex'). It is stored as an",
    "ai_draft gloss when a request is answered, so an editor who reads Norwegian must",
    "approve it before the sentence can publish.",
    "",
    "Four to nine requests per skill for greet-and-introduce; one or two for later skills."
  ],
